  96:main.c        **** // According to spwg, there's room for at least one "alternate timing"
  97:main.c        **** // This could be useful here, for saving a couple values to try-out...
  98:main.c        **** // e.g. 57Hz refresh worked perfectly, as far as I could tell, but I wanted
GAS LISTING /tmp/cc0SE7YY.s 			page 5


  99:main.c        **** // to see if I could bump that up... 60Hz looked great, until I was working
 100:main.c        **** // in Terminal (lots of contrast), and I noticed a few lines would skip...
 101:main.c        **** // Hopefully, if this works, having an alternate-timing of the
 102:main.c        **** // previously-working values will make switching back easy...
 103:main.c        **** // Haven't yet figured out how I'm going to implement this...
 104:main.c        **** // Alternate-timing kinda makes sense as the reliable fall-back
 105:main.c        **** // OTOH, if the main timing is set to something non-bootable, then what?
 106:main.c        **** // (If the experimental timing is put here in ALT_TIMING, then it could be
 107:main.c        **** // tested *after* booting with reliable values... OTOH, I've *also* found
 108:main.c        **** // that (maybe) the display itself has a better time syncing if it'd
 109:main.c        **** // already synced since power-up... so switching to a new (questionable)
 110:main.c        **** // value may work after the system has booted, but may not work upon a
 111:main.c        **** // fresh boot... 
